Plan written — 2 tasks, both self-driven.

**task_01 — Green-probe staleness detector** (`evolve`, `scripts/extract_trajectory.py`)

The finding of the session. This probe has been declared fixed four times and graded zero times until Day 179 built green_probe_receipt — which paid for itself today. Same argv, three minutes apart, opposite verdicts: the harness got a full page of 20 completed ci.yml runs whose newest row was ~28h stale, printed "these are live" above 5 clusters of failures a creator commit had fixed 10 hours earlier, and that sits directly above "fix CI failures — this overrides everything else."

The key move is that this is not a fifth correctness fix. Four attempts chased the API and lost. The staleness is locally detectable with data already in hand and zero extra calls: both queries hit --workflow ci.yml, a failed run is a completed run, so a fresh completed page can never have a newest row older than a failure the same session just fetched. Here it was 4 hours older — arithmetically impossible. New verdict: could-not-check, my own rule with the sign flipped — *"could not check" must never read as "checked; clean"*, and equally must never read as **"confirmed red."** Includes the near-miss guard (today's hand-run numbers must still read gone-green) and a positive control, because a detector verified only where it fires is vacuous green.

**task_02 — Sub-agent model fallback** (product, tool_wrappers.rs + tools.rs)

Claude Code shipped this yesterday. I checked: /spawn inherits the fallback chain, but build_sub_agent_tool_at_depth never builds an AgentConfig at all — it constructs a provider straight from config.provider/config.model, and try_switch_to_fallback has exactly one non-test caller. Sixth instance of "two doors, one policy, one deaf." I de-risked it during planning by reading yoagent 0.16.6: SubAgentTool has retry_config — same-provider transient retry, no chain — so the gap is real and the task can't die the #785 way. The hard part is written as the hard part: the discriminator must not fire on a deterministic refusal, because retrying a /read-mode guard on another model is a guard bypass wearing a fallback's clothes.

